.TH GETPASS 3
.SH NAME
getpass \- read a password
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <unistd.h>
.PP
.B char *getpass(const char *prompt);
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Getpass
reads a password from the file
.IR /dev/tty ,
or if that cannot be opened, from the standard input,
after prompting with the null-terminated string
.I prompt
and disabling echoing.
A pointer is returned to a null-terminated string
of at most 8 characters.
.PP
.B Note:
the prompt goes out through
.IR fputs (3)
here.  v7 passed the caller's string to
.IR fprintf (3)
as a
.I format
\- harmless in 1979, and a hole worth closing.
.SH FILES
/dev/tty
.br
share/besm6/lib/libc.a, lib/libc/stdio/getpass.c
.SH "SEE ALSO"
crypt(3), getpwent(3)
.SH BUGS
The return value points to static data
whose content is overwritten by each call.
.PP
.B There is no test for this routine,
and there cannot easily be one: it opens /dev/tty and would sit there waiting for
someone to type, which a harness that diffs a program's output against a recorded
expectation cannot arrange.  That is also why the bug below went unnoticed for as
long as it did.
.SH BESM-6 NOTES
.SS The bound on the copy is an index, and that was not tidying up
v7 wrote
.PP
.in +4
for (p = pbuf; ...) if (p < &pbuf[8]) *p++ = c;
.in -4
.PP
and
.B `<' between two char * values does not order them on this machine.
A fat pointer carries its byte offset in bits 47..45 and its word address in bits
15..1, and
.B the offset decrements as the pointer advances;
there is no relational helper for the general case, so the comparison reduces to
an integer comparison of the whole word and the offset field dominates the
address field.
.PP
.I Pbuf
is nine characters, so
.I p
starts at byte #0 of its first word (offset field 5) while
.B &pbuf[8]
is byte #2 of the second (offset field 3).
.B The test was false on the very first iteration:
nothing was ever stored, and
.I getpass
returned the empty string
.I every time.
.PP
It is
.B "if (n < 8) pbuf[n++] = c"
now.  See the same hazard in libtermcap and libcurses, whose ports had four and
eleven of these to remove.
.SS Dispositions are void (*)(int)
The signal it saves and restores across the read is stored in that type, which is
what <signal.h> declares and what the kernel's frame delivers.  v7 wrote
.BR "int (*)()" .
